 34MG c  Advantages of using two-step fragmentations of {+9}Be({+40}Ar,X) and   
 34MG2c  {+9}Be({+36}Si,X): If the primary {+40}Ar is used as the incident      
 34MG3c  particle, the fraction of the |g-rays from {+34}Mg in the total |g     
 34MG4c  yield is quite small due to the small production cross-section. This   
 34MG5c  leads to the use of high beam target luminosity and hence to a heavy   
 34MG6c  load from |g-ray detection. In the two-step scheme, on the other hand, 
 34MG7c  the fragmentation is induced by a nuclide close to {+34}Mg ({+36}Si in 
 34MG8c  this case). Its large production cross-section allows the use of much  
 34MG9c  more intense primary beams. As shown in fig. 5, the                    
 34MGAc  Doppler-corrected2002Mo35.                                             
 34MG c  2001Yo03, 2002Yo04: a 95-MeV/nucleon, 60-pnA primary beam of {+40}Ar   
 34MG2c  bombarded a 462.5-mg/cm2 {+9}Be primary target. A secondary beam of    
 34MG3c  {+36}Si was separated by the RIKEN projectile-fragment separator RIPS  
 34MG4c  and transported to a secondary target of 385.5-mg/cm2 {+9}Be at a      
 34MG5c  center-of-target energy of 38 MeV/nucleon. Reaction products from the  
 34MG6c  {+36}Si+{+9}Be fragmentation were detected and identified at forward   
 34MG7c  angles with a parallel plate avalanche counter (PPAC) and four sets of 
 34MG8c  counter telescopes, each consisting of three silicon detectors and one 
 34MG9c  Si(Li) detector. |g rays emitted from excited fragments were detected  
 34MGAc  using 66 NaI(Tl) detectors. Measured E|g with Doppler correction, I|g, 
 34MGBc  ({+34}Mg)|g-coin, and |g|g-coin. Deduced levels. Also see review       
 34MGCc  articles 2002Sa11 and 2002Mo35.                                        
